        <body>
            <div xml:id="d514">

                <pb facs="1040" n="1010" sameAs="frus:frus1947v01/pg1010"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">560.AL/10–1147: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Troutman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-10-11T12:00:00+01:00">October 11,
                            1947—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1118. Personal for Clayton and Wilcox only from Brown. Helmore told me privately
                    last night that decision refuse colonial concession <pb facs="1041" n="1011"
                        xml:id="pg1011"/> was personal decision Bevin based on fear political
                    reaction in UK and colonies. He strongly hinted we should tell Inverchapel we
                    are not convinced by British reasons for refusing colonial concession and should
                    insist on getting it. Other alternatives Helmore hinted open to us were insist
                    substantial other concession equivalent value colonial concession or refuse bind
                    rubber if no colonial concession offered.</p>
                <p>Wilgress saw Helmore last night and advises us insist obtain colonial concession
                    on grounds reasons given by UK for refusal unconvincing. British and Canadians
                    most anxious we should indicate in some way in this agreement willingness
                    negotiate our mixing regulations. Wilgress feels our insistence colonial
                    concession will forestall British efforts link some other concession our mixing
                    regulations.</p>
                <p>Reporting TAC reaction Frito series. My personal opinion is that in light overall picture
                    latest British action is comparatively of minor economic significance but makes
                    bur political problem somewhat more difficult. Development reported mytel 1100, October 8,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d514-f1"/> infinitely more important. Wilgress says this now “75
                    percent sure.” Difficulties reported your 1284, October 7,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d514-f2"/> indicate we might be as well off with no colonial offer
                    and nothing linked our mixing regulations. I would settle this basis.</p>
                <p>Now expect all negotiations be concluded October 15 with signature agreement just
                    before end month. Prompt decision obviously of utmost importance. Will stand by
                    for messages all weekend. [Brown.]</p>
                <closer>[File copy unsigned]</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
